### What is ScopedValue?

`ScopedValue` binds an immutable value to a scope and makes it available to all code executing within that scope. The binding is automatically removed when the scope exits. It is the modern replacement for `ThreadLocal` in virtual-thread code.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.ScopedValue;

public class RequestContext {

    public static final ScopedValue<String> CURRENT_USER = ScopedValue.newInstance();
    public static final ScopedValue<String> CORRELATION_ID = ScopedValue.newInstance();

    public void handleRequest(String user, String correlationId) {
        ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, user)
            .where(CORRELATION_ID, correlationId)
            .run(() -> {
                // All code in this block can read CURRENT_USER and CORRELATION_ID
                processTransfer();
            });
        // Bindings automatically removed here
    }

    private void processTransfer() {
        String user = CURRENT_USER.get();          // "Rajesh"
        String corrId = CORRELATION_ID.get();      // "TXN-2024-001"
        System.out.println(corrId + ": Processing transfer for " + user);
    }
}
```

### Why is ThreadLocal problematic for virtual threads?

ThreadLocal has four problems when used with millions of virtual threads.

First, memory leaks: ThreadLocal values persist until explicitly removed with `remove()`. Forgetting to call `remove()` leaks memory. With millions of virtual threads, this leak is amplified. Second, mutable state: ThreadLocal allows `set()` at any time, making it hard to reason about what value a method sees. Third, expensive inheritance: `InheritableThreadLocal` copies values to child threads, which is costly with millions of virtual threads. Fourth, pool pollution: in thread pools, a ThreadLocal set by one task may be visible to the next task if `remove()` is missed.

```java
// WRONG: ThreadLocal with virtual threads
ThreadLocal<String> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            currentUser.set("Rajesh");     // mutable!
            processRequest();
            // currentUser.remove();        // easy to forget!
        });
    }
}
// 1,000,000 ThreadLocal entries if remove() is missed

// CORRECT: ScopedValue with virtual threads
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, "Rajesh").run(() -> {
                processRequest();
            });
            // Automatically cleaned, no remove() needed
        });
    }
}
```

### How is ScopedValue different from ThreadLocal?

| Aspect | ThreadLocal | ScopedValue |
|---|---|---|
| Mutability | Mutable (set() anytime) | Immutable within scope |
| Cleanup | Manual remove() required | Automatic on scope exit |
| Inheritance | Copies per child thread | Efficient sharing |
| Memory risk | Leak-prone (forgotten remove) | Leak-free by design |
| Thread model | Designed for platform threads | Designed for virtual threads |
| API | get(), set(), remove() | get(), isBound() (read-only) |
| Structured concurrency | Not compatible | Inherited by forked subtasks |

### How does ScopedValue work with Structured Concurrency?

When you fork subtasks inside a StructuredTaskScope, the ScopedValue bindings from the parent scope are automatically available to the child subtasks.

```java
public void handleTransfer(String user, String accountId) {
    ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, user).run(() -> {
        try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
            scope.fork(() -> {
                // CURRENT_USER.get() returns "Rajesh" here
                return checkBalance(accountId);
            });
            scope.fork(() -> {
                // CURRENT_USER.get() returns "Rajesh" here too
                return checkFraud(accountId);
            });
            scope.join();
            scope.throwIfFailed();
        }
    });
}
```

### What are the rules for using ScopedValue?

- Declare as `static final`: `public static final ScopedValue<String> KEY = ScopedValue.newInstance();`
- Bind with `ScopedValue.where(KEY, value).run(task)` or `.call(callable)`.
- Read with `KEY.get()` (throws NoSuchElementException if not bound) or `KEY.isBound()`.
- You cannot call `set()` because the value is immutable within its scope.
- Nested `where()` calls can rebind the same key for a narrower scope.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: reading outside scope
ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, "Rajesh").run(() -> processRequest());
String user = CURRENT_USER.get();  // NoSuchElementException: not bound!

// WRONG: trying to mutate
ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, "Rajesh").run(() -> {
    // CURRENT_USER.set("Priya");  // No set() method exists!
});

// CORRECT: rebind for a narrower scope
ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, "Rajesh").run(() -> {
    System.out.println(CURRENT_USER.get());  // "Rajesh"
    ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, "Priya").run(() -> {
        System.out.println(CURRENT_USER.get());  // "Priya" (rebound)
    });
    System.out.println(CURRENT_USER.get());  // "Rajesh" (restored)
});
```

### When should you still use ThreadLocal?

ThreadLocal remains appropriate when you need mutable per-thread state on platform threads, such as thread-local caches, thread-local random number generators, or framework internals that require mutation.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- ScopedValue was finalized in JDK 25 after previews in JDK 20-24.
- The binding is stored in the virtual thread's context, not in a hash map like ThreadLocal.
- Rebinding a ScopedValue in a nested scope creates a new binding that shadows the outer one; the outer binding is restored when the inner scope exits.
- For carrier cost: ScopedValue has near-zero overhead for reads because the JVM can inline the lookup.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can ScopedValue replace all ThreadLocal uses? (No. ThreadLocal is still needed for mutable per-thread state and in platform-thread-pool scenarios.)
- What if I need to pass mutable state? (Use a final reference to a mutable object, or pass it as a method parameter.)
- How is ScopedValue different from passing parameters? (ScopedValue avoids threading context through every method signature in deep call chains.)
